+Trace the resolved client version, service configuration, wrappers/interceptors and completion
+callbacks to the event that commits a winner. Native transport hedging need not evaluate the
+application's freshness or validity predicate. For example, grpc-java 1.76.0
+`RetriableStream.headersRead` commits an attempt and schedules cancellation of competing streams
+before delivering response headers upstream. A stale payload rejected later cannot revive them;
+even a configured nonfatal status arriving after headers cannot undo commitment.
+
+Native hedging can remain appropriate when eligibility is enforced before that commitment and
+the failure policy matches the contract. With gRPC, an error sent before response headers/messages
+can be trailers-only; configure which such statuses permit continuing the race. Otherwise move
+eligibility earlier or own a bounded application race that preserves eligible attempts, using
+one hedge owner and the existing deadline/admission budget. Merely adding a validator after
+the native call or stacking another independent hedge does not implement that policy.
+
